<h2>A Year in the Life of an Austin Roof</h2>
<p>Residential roofing in Austin is shaped by a climate that attacks roofs from every direction. Knowing what each season does helps you spot problems early.</p>
<h3>Spring: hail and straight-line winds</h3>
<p>March through June brings most of the region’s severe thunderstorms. Hail bruises shingles and knocks off the protective granules, and gusts lift shingle tabs and break their seal strips. Damage is often invisible from the ground, which is why a free inspection after a significant storm is worth scheduling even if nothing is leaking.</p>
<h3>Summer: heat and UV</h3>
<p>Long stretches over 100°F bake the asphalt in shingles, dry out sealants and crack rubber pipe boots. A poorly ventilated attic makes it worse, cooking shingles from below. This is the season when roofs quietly lose years of life.</p>
<h3>Fall: debris and heavy rain</h3>
<p>Live oak leaves, cedar debris and pecan litter collect in valleys and gutters, holding water against the roof. Fall downpours then find any weak flashing that summer heat opened up.</p>
<h3>Winter: occasional freezes</h3>
<p>Central Texas freezes are infrequent but can be severe, as homeowners saw in February 2021. Ice can back up at eaves and burst plumbing in attics, and freeze-thaw cycles stress already-brittle shingles.</p>

<h2>From Bungalows to New Subdivisions</h2>
<p>Austin’s housing stock is unusually varied, and each type brings its own roofing quirks. Older homes in central and <a href="/service-areas/east-austin/">East Austin</a> neighborhoods often have multiple roof layers, board decking with gaps, and low-slope porch roofs added decades later. Mid-century ranch homes in <a href="/service-areas/south-austin/">South Austin</a> frequently have undersized attic ventilation. Newer subdivisions in Round Rock, Pflugerville and Kyle tend to have complex rooflines with many valleys, plus HOA rules about shingle color and metal accents. Hill Country homes face more wind exposure and, on some lots, fire-rating requirements. As an Austin residential roofing contractor, we adjust our approach to the house in front of us rather than applying one template to every roof.</p>

<h2>How to Choose an Austin Residential Roofing Company</h2>
<p>After a big hailstorm, out-of-town crews and door knockers flood Austin neighborhoods, and it can be hard to tell who will still be around if your roof leaks next year. Whoever you hire, these questions separate a dependable residential roofer from the rest:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Are you local, and can I reach you after the job?</strong> A real local phone number and email matter when you need warranty service or a storm repair.</li>
  <li><strong>Are you insured?</strong> Ask for proof of liability and workers’ compensation or occupational accident coverage before anyone climbs on your roof.</li>
  <li><strong>Will you show me photos?</strong> A trustworthy roofer documents the problems they find instead of asking you to take their word for it.</li>
  <li><strong>Is the quote itemized?</strong> Tear-off, decking, underlayment, flashing, ventilation and cleanup should all be spelled out.</li>
  <li><strong>Will you install to the manufacturer’s specifications?</strong> Shortcuts on nailing or ventilation can void the shingle warranty. Our <a href="/blog/residential-roofing-warranty-coverage-guide-for-homeowners/">roofing warranty guide</a> explains why.</li>
  <li><strong>Do you pressure me to sign today?</strong> Storm deadlines are real, but a good contractor gives you time to compare options.</li>
</ul>
<p>Austin Roofing is happy to answer every one of those questions. We would rather earn your trust with a free inspection and an honest recommendation than with a high-pressure pitch on your doorstep.</p>

<h2>What Working With Us Looks Like</h2>
